<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern and Southern dynasties</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/420">420</a>–<a href="/years/589">589</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Northern and Southern dynasties was a period of political division in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 420 to 589, following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> and the Eastern Jin dynasty. It began when Liu Yu seized the throne and founded the Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>; in the south the Liu Song was followed by the Southern Qi, Liang and Chen, while in the north the Northern Wei, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> ethnicity, gave way to the Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>. The age saw civil war, large-scale migration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>. It ended when Emperor Wen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> unified China proper in 589.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northern-and-southern-dynasties.jpg" alt="Northern and Southern dynasties" width="220" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Wu_of_Chen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liu Yu is regarded as one of the best generals of the <b>Northern and Southern dynasties</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liu-song">Liu Song · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sui rose out of the military aristocracy of north-western China that had formed during the long division of the <b>Northern and Southern dynasties</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The first commentaries date from the <b>Northern and Southern dynasties</b> (420 to 589) and the early Tang dynasty, and most historical editions include those of Pei Yin (5th century), Sima Zhen and Zhang Shoujie (both early 8th century).</blockquote><a href="/work/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
